WM(1)                          P32W Unix                           WM(1)

NAME
       wm - windows on the video screen, a terminal in each

SYNOPSIS
       wm [-g COLSxROWS] [-s SCRIPT]

DESCRIPTION
       wm takes the video card's screen, the mouse and the keyboard
       and puts windows on a desk. Every window is a terminal with a
       shell in it, as if it were another line into the machine:
       the programs in different windows run at once, and what one
       writes while its window is covered is there when the window
       is uncovered.

       It starts with one terminal. The keys typed go to the window
       in front, the one whose bar is white.

THE MOUSE
       The left button does everything:

       in a window         brings it to the front
       on a window's bar   the same, and held down it drags the
                           window: an outline follows the pointer,
                           and the window is put there when the
                           button comes up
       on the box at the left of a bar
                           closes the window. Its programs are hung
                           up, as when a line drops
       on Desk, on the bar across the top
                           lets the menu down while the button is
                           held; the item it is let go on is chosen

       The Desk menu has: New terminal (of the size -g gives, 64
       columns by 16 rows without it); Big terminal (80 by 24);
       Repaint (the whole screen made again); Quit (every window
       closed, and the console's text back).

PICTURES
       A program that draws with the graphics library (gfx(3):
       cc ... -lgfx, and BASIC's SCREEN 1) gets a window of its
       own for its picture when it is run in a terminal here:
       Picture 1, and so on. Nothing in the program is different.
       The picture is 400 by 300 dots unless WMSIZE says another
       size:

              WMSIZE=320x200 basic plot.bas

       The keys go to the program's terminal when its picture is
       in front. The picture's window is closed when the program
       closes its screen or ends, or by its box (the program is
       not stopped by that; what it draws then goes nowhere). The
       picture of a program that was killed stays until then, or
       until the next program on that terminal draws. One picture
       to a terminal.

       win(1) starts a program in a window of its own, with a
       terminal to itself: the way to have a clock, or anything
       that draws, beside the terminals and not in one.

TERMINALS
       A window's terminal understands what the console's screen
       does (the same control characters and ESC [ sequences), so
       whatever runs there runs in a window: vi, top, more, basic.
       LINES and COLUMNS are set to the window's size, which those
       programs go by. A window whose shell ends is closed.

       The windows' terminals are the pseudo-terminals /dev/ttyp4
       to /dev/ttyp15 (ps shows them as 9 to 20): 12 windows at
       most. Only root and group staff may open them, as the
       screen and the mouse.

OPTIONS
       -g COLSxROWS   the size of a terminal: 20 to 80 columns by 4
                      to 28 rows
       -s SCRIPT      a hand is played from the file SCRIPT, and wm
                      ends when the file does: for showing, and for
                      the system's tests. Its lines:
                      key TEXT        the keys of TEXT typed (\n is
                                      Return, \e ESC, \\ a \)
                      mouse X Y       the pointer to the screen's
                                      dot X, Y (0, 0 the bottom-left)
                      press, release  the left button
                      wait TEXT       until the line the front
                                      window's cursor is on ends
                                      with TEXT: a prompt (or the
                                      line above, if the cursor is
                                      at the start of its own)
                      pause N         N 50ths of a second with the
                                      hand still
                      dump            the windows and their text,
                                      printed when wm ends
                      quit

EXAMPLES
       wm
       wm -g 80x24
       wm -s /usr/examples/wm.hand
              a hand that opens two terminals and uses them

FILES
       /dev/video, /dev/mouse, /dev/ptyp*, /dev/ttyp*
       /usr/src/cmd/wm.c, wmwin.c, wmterm.c, wmgfx.c   its source

SEE ALSO
       win(1), gfx(3), mouse(4), video(4), sh(1)

DIAGNOSTICS
       wm: Device busy when another program has the screen or the
       mouse; wm: No such device or address without the video
       card. Only root and group staff may use them.

BUGS
       A window cannot be made bigger or smaller once it is open.
       A program that asks about its picture (the mouse, a dot)
       must be in the foreground of its terminal (gfx(3)), or be
       started with win(1).
       The keys come from the terminal wm was started on.
